{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":5,"timestamp":1786797050,"nonce":"DHnQ897MUTZTerh+sVVMF76LbBI=","metadata":{"github-id":"LE_lADORPa0Bs8AAAABM4Wp1s8AAAAG3kgVuQ"},"added":["bug"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"timestamp":1787263234,"nonce":"IEMNnDNDA/8QSccyA5AQiQiYdaM=","metadata":{"github-id":"IC_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABP6Brpw","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/100#issuecomment-5362445223"},"message":"Fixed in #117. The cause is not an eval-side cache, and it is not specific to `fem.solve()`.\n\n`jno.core(...)` reads `layer.module` once at construction, through `build_single_layer_params`, and evaluates its own copy from then on. `crux.models` holds no reference to `g.model`, so a later in-place swap is invisible. Evaluating the parameter directly shows it plainly — the core never saw 1 or 5, it saw the value `g` held when the core was built:\n\n```\ng=1: stored=[1.]  eval(g*1.0)=[0.]  sol.mean=0.022491\ng=5: stored=[5.]  eval(g*1.0)=[0.]  sol.mean=0.022491\n```\n\nThat also explains why the DD data-field tests work: they mutate and then call `fem.solve()` / `fdm.solve()` **eagerly**, with no core in the path, so the module is read at solve time.\n\nOf the two options in the issue, refuse is the right one. Honouring the live module in `eval` would patch only `eval` — `self.models`, what training starts from and what every other read uses, would still hold the old value, so eval and solve would then disagree about the same parameter; and it would skip the build a module goes through on the way in (pretrained weights, a callable initializer, an initialize mask, a dtype cast, sharding).\n\nOne subtlety worth recording: `solve()` syncs trained weights back onto `layer.module`, so a check against the module the core was *built* from refuses every eval that follows a solve. The check tracks the module each layer carried when the core **last** read it, refreshed at that write-back.\n\n**Adjacent, not fixed in #117:** `crux.solve()` starts from `dict(self.models)`, the same snapshot, so a module swapped before solve is silently ignored there too. `tests/test_integration.py:289` installs a paramax wrapper after its core is built and then asserts training yields a finite loss — the wrapper never reaches the forward path, so that test passes whether or not auto-unwrapping works. Extending the guard to `solve` would surface it, but it needs that test restructured, so it belongs in its own change.","files":null},{"type":4,"timestamp":1787306391,"nonce":"UWpFlTqNNovydJqn9AzIBtpHFv4=","metadata":{"github-id":"CE_lADORPa0Bs8AAAABM4Wp1s8AAAAG8BILhQ"},"status":2}]}